    {"issue": "This replacement strips fungi and legionella out of \"pollution condition\" while Definitions V.C. (certified industrial hygienist), V.L. (fungi) and V.Y. (remediation costs, which prescribes how fungi and legionella remediation costs are established) remain in force. Whether those provisions retain any operation cannot be answered without reading them against this text.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c180", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The added exclusion bars claims and costs related to \"fungi\", legionella \"or odor\", although the same endorsement has already removed fungi, legionella and odor from the definition of \"pollution condition\". Whether the exclusion does independent work — for example reaching conditions that also involve other contaminants — turns on reading the two paragraphs together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c181", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The $500,000 natural resource damage sublimit applies to \"claims\" and associated legal defense expense, while Definition V.X.4. makes natural resource damages a species of \"property damage\" subject to the Item 2.b./3.b. per-condition limits. Which cap applies to a claim mixing natural resource damage with other property damage is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c182", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c183", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The grant covers only \"claims\" and associated legal defense expense arising out of product pollution, while the endorsement's Limits of Liability section caps \"claims\", \"remediation costs\" and legal defense expense arising out of product pollution. Whether remediation costs for product pollution are covered at all cannot be told from the two read together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c184", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Paragraph 2. contains the duplicated phrase \"on or after the on or after the Retroactive Date, if any\", and \"first commences, in its entirety\" is applied to \"product pollution\" whose defining event is a third party's later use of the product. When product pollution \"commences\" is left indeterminate by the wording.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c185", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This section makes the Aggregate Product Pollution Sublimit the maximum for \"claims\", \"remediation costs\" and legal defense expense, though the insuring clause grants no remediation-cost coverage for product pollution, and the per-condition and aggregate sublimits are both $1,000,000 so that the per-condition cap has no independent effect. Whether remediation costs are within this coverage depends on reconciling the two.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c186", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The exclusion reaches anything \"arising out of any goods or products manufactured, sold or distributed by an 'insured'\", a causal phrase with no stated limit, with a carve-back only for \"product pollution\" as defined. Whether contamination at the insured's premises \"arises out of\" its products is left open by the breadth of the phrase.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c187", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Product Pollution\" requires a pollution condition arising out of \"the use of a product ... after the 'insured' has relinquished possession\", and then says \"products do not include waste products\" without defining waste. Whether a contaminated feed, egg or by-product is a product or a waste product decides the coverage.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c188", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c189", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The endorsement says sanctions may \"prohibit us from providing insurance\", using \"us\" although the Policy elsewhere identifies only \"the Insurer\" and defines no first-person parties. It also supplies no mechanism for determining when a prohibition applies.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c190", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c191", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c192", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The company's promise to \"submit to the jurisdiction of any court of competent jurisdiction\" at the insured's request cannot be squared with Section IX.I., which commits the parties to the exclusive jurisdiction of the State of New York. Which provision fixes the forum is a direct conflict.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c193", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c194", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The Canadian service provision presupposes an insured resident in Canada, although Section IV. limits coverage to pollution conditions located and claims made within the United States. The provision has no evident field of operation under this Policy.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c195", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The saving clause says nothing herein varies the Policy's terms \"other than as above stated\", leaving unresolved whether the endorsement's jurisdiction and service undertakings do override Section IX.I.'s exclusive New York jurisdiction clause. The exception swallows the reservation.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
  ]}
]}

